Here, `Func<int>` represents a call with no arguments that returns an integer; `Action` represents a call with no arguments and no return value.

```csharp
int count = 2;
int snapshot = count;
Func<int> read = () => count;
Action increment = () => count++;

count = 5;
increment();
Console.WriteLine(read());
Console.WriteLine(snapshot);
```

What two numbers are printed, in order? Explain which storage `read` and `increment` access and why `snapshot` behaves as it does.

Here, `Func<int>` means a delegate that takes no arguments and returns an `int`.

```csharp
static Func<int> MakeCounter()
{
    int count = 0;
    return () => ++count;
}

var first = MakeCounter();
var second = first;

Console.WriteLine(first());
Console.WriteLine(second());
```

What does this print, and why can both calls access `count` after `MakeCounter` has returned? (`++count` increments first, then returns the new value.)

Trace this code twice: first with `fail = false`, then with `fail = true`. Each run starts fresh with `state = 0` and `result = -1`.

```csharp
bool fail = false;
int state = 0;

Func<int> pipeline = () =>
{
    state += 1;
    return state;
};

pipeline += () =>
{
    state += 10;
    if (fail)
        throw new InvalidOperationException();
    return state;
};

pipeline += () =>
{
    state += 100;
    return state;
};

int result = -1;
try
{
    result = pipeline();
}
catch (InvalidOperationException)
{
}

Console.WriteLine($"{state}, {result}");
```

What line is printed in each run? Explain which handlers run and why `result` ends with that value.

